# Whole properties: read before any house, plot or streetscape build

**The unit of work is a whole property** (user, 11 Oct 2026). Not a façade,
not a street-side shell. One property means:
- **House:** every elevation (front, sides, rear incl. back extensions) guided
  by the OSM footprint (Minecraft-readable, not a jagged 1:1 trace — see
  minecraft-craftsmanship.mdc), a real roof form (pitch, hips, gables, ridge
  direction from aerial; ridge/eaves Y from LIDAR massing), windows and doors
  on all sides, bays and porches, and chimney stacks as plain massing.
- **Inside: floors only.** One floor per storey and one stair so it is
  walkable. No rooms, partitions or furniture. Never research the inside of a
  private house.
- **Front garden:** boundary at the back of the pavement (low wall, railings or
  hedge, at most 2 high), gate gap, path to the door, grass/paving/bin store.
- **Back garden:** layout from the **aerial only**: fences/walls on plot lines,
  lawn vs patio, sheds/outbuildings (at most 4 high), trees. No garden
  furniture, no peering photos.

What went wrong before (I-009): five façade builds put a brick shell on the
pavement of Broadlands Road, ~6 blocks in front of the real houses. Readback,
walk and patrol all passed. All five builds were reverted.

## 1. Survey the street and the plots first
- `python3 scripts/osm_footprints.py frontage --bbox x0,z0,x1,z1`: each house's
  real `addr:street` + number, front-wall MC x,z, setback, and the live
  surface profile out to the road.
- `python3 scripts/osm_footprints.py plots --bbox …`: a first-guess plot per
  house (house + front garden + back garden cells, JSON + PNG). Plot sides
  continue the party walls. Move fences to where the aerial shows them.
- Name builds by **real street + numbers**, e.g. `broadlands_rd_20_22`.
  The radial focus (X7097 Z-5521) is on **Broadlands Road**. The Portswood Rd
  junction is at about MC X7198 Z-5461.
- Work a few adjacent properties per build (a semi pair or 2–4 terrace houses).
  Each build covers whole plots, so neighbours meet cleanly at the plot lines.

## 2. Placement rules
- House walls sit on the **OSM footprint as a guide** — typically within about
  1–2 blocks after squaring/simplifying for Minecraft. Never put them at
  "the last solid block before the pavement" (I-009).
- Prefer airtight shells, glass with solid flanks, and roofs shallower than a
  full stair unless evidence says otherwise (`minecraft-craftsmanship.mdc`).
- Doors face the street they are numbered on (perpendicular to the road).
- Keep Geocraft/LIDAR Y as the massing authority (ground, eaves, ridge).
  Clear Geocraft's carpet "roof" and stone undercroft where the real house
  and garden replace them.
- `site_kit.apply()` refuses building-like blocks far outside OSM footprints
  (street-side / back-garden height caps). That guard stops pavement builds,
  not careful ±1–2 block craftsmanship. Set `plan.outside_footprint_ok` only
  for a genuinely unmapped structure and name its evidence id — never to shove
  a house onto the kerb.

## 3. Describe the evidence before planning
Before planning, write 3–5 lines per property group in REVIEWS or the place
notes. Cover storeys, bays/porches, opening rhythm, materials, roof form
(aerial), front boundary + garden depth (street photo), and back-garden
layout (aerial). Name the photo/aerial id for each. Same-terrace typology
may be used as a hint for side and rear elevations. Label it `hint`.

## 4. Check the result against the evidence, not against itself
- Readback, walk and patrol prove *the blocks match the plan*, not that the
  plan matches Southampton.
- Done needs all of these:
  - `osm_footprints.py check --build <name>` passes.
  - A client view from the reference photo's camera (manifest `bbox_mc`),
    facing the same way, sits beside the photo in the contact sheet.
  - A top-down view sits beside the aerial crop.
  - 3 concrete matches/mismatches are written in REVIEWS.

## 5. Stop instead of patching
- If your own new build needs a fix build for its *geometry* (blocked doors,
  walls in the road, kerbs on brick), stop. That is a survey error. Revert,
  re-survey, re-plan. Do not stack _b/_c/_d patches.
- If a QUEUE item tells you to repeat a method that contradicts the evidence,
  fix the queue item and log an ISSUE instead of following it.
